  "interpretation": {
    "observation": "Positive initialization changed the final learned bias relative to zero-bias SReLU but did not materially change the final negative-gate fraction. Both biased runs retained zero preactivation gradients at every sampled negative-gate position.",
    "mechanism": "The trainable biases moved below zero by the final checkpoint, and the surrounding network adapted so gate means became more negative. The small reduction in sampled-never-positive channels did not reduce the upstream gradient energy routed into the clipped half-space.",
    "outcome": "Both bias candidates were slower to the fixed 95 percent target and lost peak accuracy relative to zero-bias SReLU. Their small wall-time reductions came from run-speed variation and did not compensate for worse convergence or quality.",
    "direction": "Reject positive initialization alone. If bias remains in scope, test a mechanism that preserves a positive operating point during training, such as a constrained or separately parameterized offset, before broad dropout or AdamW tuning.",
    "limitations": [
      "This is a cross-sectional final-checkpoint diagnostic and does not show when the learned biases crossed zero.",
      "A sampled-never-positive channel was nonpositive only across these images, masks, and tokens; it is not proven globally dead.",
      "CPU float32 diagnostics differ from the GPU bfloat16 autocast used during training.",
      "Each bias value has one seed, so run-speed and endpoint differences are directional rather than replicated."
    ]
  },
